  "query_preview": "..\n\nSurface high pressure is locked in from southern Quebec across the \nGreat Lakes which has dry and stable conditions entrenched across \nLower Mi today. A classic early fall day is the result with \ntemperatures near normal and wind speed down a notch compared to \nWednesday. Early day shallow cumulus is long gone by mid afternoon \nallowing full sun to lift temperatures to highs around 70. Clear sky \nand light wind follow tonight which set up favorable radiational \ncooling conditions as high pressure is centered more overhead \ncompared to recent days. This supports guidance offering lows in the \nlower 40s over a large area of SE Mi outside of metro Detroit. One \nor two upper 30s reports are also in reach from the typically cooler \nlocations around SE Mi by sunrise Friday.  \n\nDouble checking upstream conditions today shows a band of showers \nextending from the upper Midwest into the central Plains associated \nwith a weak mid level wave and surface trough. Model projections \nshow this system quickly overwhelmed by larger scale ridge \ndevelopment as the long wave blocking pattern shifts configuration \ntonight and Friday. The moisture axis is reduced to a band of mixed \nhigh clouds tracking across the central Great Lakes Friday afternoon \nand evening. Dry and stable conditions are then solidified during \nthe weekend as the mid/upper level ridge and surface high pressure \nstrengthen over the Great Lakes while the nor'easter matures on the \nmid Atlantic coast. \n\nConsensus of extended time range models indicates changes beginning \nMonday as the nor'easter moves slowly up the coast Monday night and \nTuesday. This promotes deamplification of the trailing mid/upper \nlevel ridge over the Great Lakes in a transition toward greater \ninfluence of the westerlies that carry multiple short waves across \nCanada during the mid week period. It takes until Wednesday for the \nlead wave and surface system to reach the Great Lakes and bring the \nnext chance of rain to Lower Mi. Models also produce a cut-off low \naround the 4 Corners to southern Plains which is positioned to \nintercept much of the tropical moisture from \"Polo\" remnants mid to \nlate next week.",
